hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to talk  about coupled transcription and  translation now I think that these are  processes you've probably heard of  before really quick let's just review  what they mean when the cell is  synthesizing in RNA as I'm called an  mRNA message from a DNA template this is  what happens in the process of  transcription when the mRNA synthesized  and transcription is then used as a  template for the synthesis of protein by  the cell this is what's happening in a  process of translation now these two  processes together are the major  processes of gene expression in the cell  when I made gene expression I mean the  information that is contained in this in  the gene in the genetic information in  this DNA of the cell it is being  expressed by way of an mRNA message that  has been translated during translation  to make protein and the proteins are the  functional units within the cell that  are carrying out the activities that are  encoded for by those genes and so these  processes transcription and translation  are the two major processes in gene  expression and we learn about them with  the central dogma and so if you're  interested in learning more about gene  expression and what it entails within  the central dogma you see my video on  that topic so now let's get back to this  idea of couples transcription and  translation and what that means now in  eukaryotes  remember that eukaryotes are all  organisms that are not prokaryotes so as  we mean by that there are differences  between eukaryotes and prokaryotes  oMG today the new carriers were mainly  thinking about animals including humans  also plants fungi algae things like that  and so in eukaryotes these two processes  transcription and translation are  separated both in time and in space and  what I mean by that is that eukaryotes  have a nuclear membrane that houses the  DNA so in all eukaryotic cells  transcription is happening where the DNA  is inside the nucleus translation is  happening in the cytoplasm outside the  nucleus and those transcription has to  complete first and then the completed  mRNA message actually gets a post  transcriptionally modified to make  what's called a mature mRNA transcript  if you're interested in learning more  about that post-transcriptional  modification process that occurs between  transcription and translation in  eukaryotes please see my video on that  topic and then once that mature  transcript has been completely modified  it then leaves the nucleus goes out into  the cytoplasm it's translated so in  eukaryotes transcription and translation  they have in a different places and  transcription happens first and then  translation happens second in  prokaryotes on the other hand which  includes all bacteria as well as a type  of cell called archaea so in prokaryotes  there is no nuclear membrane and so the  DNA is just free-floating in the  cytoplasm it is typically constrained to  a regular region of the cell but there's  still no nuclear membrane is completely  compartmentalizing and so there's no  nuclear membrane to separate these two  processes which means the transcription  and translation can actually be  occurring simultaneously and this is  what we say is coupling so the fact that  there's no nuclear membrane to separate  these two processes that is what allows  them to be coupled  couples just meaning that they are  occurring at the same time so now let's  look at what this looks like here in red  we have the DNA when it's being  transcribed the two strands of DNA  difficult part and an enzyme called RNA  polymerase that's here in blue there  right RNA halt for RNA polymerase it is  moving down the DNA creating mRNA here  this brown line coming off the RNA  polymerase is the mRNA and again this  red one up here is DNA so this mRNA is  being made by the RNA polymerase  remember that that is transcription so  this gene in the DNA is being  transcribed at the same time as the RNA  polymerase is sterile transcribing this  gene already you have ribosomes and the  ribosomes are here in orange each one is  an individual writers own from here I  have 1 2 3 4 5 6 drawn and that can be  many more so each individual ribosome is  already synthesizing proteins and that  is what is here shown in pink and this  is translations and this is a protein  and so you have all of these ping  polypeptides change ready for what make  up proteins and so as the other  ribosomes dots on the mRNA it starts  moving this way and as they get closer  and closer to where the RNA polymerase  is working they're making longer and  longer polypeptides now all of these  polypeptides are eventually going to be  the same protein because the the  ribosomes are translating the same gene  along this mRNA so here's a cell is  making many copies of the same protein  and you just see the size difference  here just one a singer simply because  this writer zone is further down the NRA  and so further along that process of  translation to say this one is for  example now when you come all of these  ribosomes working through side by side  although they are working independently  we actually call this right here a poly  ribosome or sometimes a poly film and  that is refers to the fact that there  are multiple ribosomes working at once  and just to reiterate they are  translating this protein translating the  mRNA into protein before this RNA  polymerase is even done transcribing of  course it does have to finish  transcribing before they can complete  their polypeptide synthesis but still  the processes are happening basically at  the same time and I want to point out  one more thing prokaryotic transcripts  tend to have many genes per transcript  not so the eukaryotes we usually only  have one gene on each mRNA transcripts  so prokaryotic transcript meaning the  mRNA can have many distinct unions on  them this is known as what is called up  polycistronic so polycistronic this  meaning that within one long mRNA  transcript there can be ribosomes that  are translating along different parts of  it resulting in different protein so  that is it today for couples  transcription and translation thank you  for watching
 

